  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\n Currently, we are sitting in between a weak trough to our south,  \nand a weak ridge trying to develop to our north. While both seem \nto be struggling, model guidance suggests both can fully develop \nby Friday morning. Between these two features, the area is \nexperiencing an easterly/northeasterly flow. This pattern is \naccompanied by significantly slower winds in the northern portions\nof the forecast area, closer to the high, and faster flow in the \nsouthern regions, near the low. Residual moisture being trapped by\nthe two systems seems to be keeping the temperatures in check for\nthe moment. Limited positive moisture advection is present when \nlooking at the higher elevations, with a moderate moisture \ntransport moving towards northern Nevada out of the Great Salt \nLake. As this is all near the surface, the upper levels will still\nremain dry from the overlying ridge, allowing us to remain in \nminimal cloud cover for the next few days. It is for these reasons\nwe will see a gradual warming into pleasant temperatures as we \nwork through the weekend. The highs will be sitting in the 60s and\n70s as we move through today, and will increase at very slow \nrates as we get to Monday. Our lows are currently expected to stay\nin the low 30s tonight, but if recent trends continue, I wouldn\u2019t\nbe surprised to see them land in the mid 30s. Not a major \ndifference, but enough to acknowledge the models are overshooting \nthe lows due to the higher dew points. These lows are expected to \nincrease up to the upper 30s, lower 40s as we get to Sunday before\nthe next pattern sets in. \n\nNext week, we are still expecting a major polar jet to push\nthrough, bringing a very deep low, colder temperatures, and\nprecipitation. Who needs wishcasting when you can have this. Long\nrange model guidance is showing a deep low moving off the Pacific\nOcean across portions of AZ, CA, and southern NV, which will\nextend a vorticity maxima line across the state with the passage.\nThe different models are still struggling with timing, but there\nis an overall trend of moving it later into the forecast period.\nThe GFS currently has the primary passage occurring for the\nTuesday into Wednesday time frame, while the ECMWF is looking more\nmid-day Thursday into Friday. Precipitation types are still a\nquestion, with the biggest concerns being around the surface. We\nare currently looking at surface temps hovering around freezing.\nAs of right now, the less whimsical option appears more likely, in\nthe surface lows will stay above freezing until after the FROPA\nand the precipitation has passed. This means widespread rain is in\nthe forecast with potential for snow mostly in the higher\nelevations. This does not mean that we won\u2019t see any snow in the\nvalleys, but the likelihood of just rain is still seeming higher.\nIf one thing is certain, this is not an event to leave town for if\nyou love winter. As we push into this event, the highs are\nsupposed to be in the upper 50s/ low 60s pre-frontal, and in the\nmid 40s/ low 50s post-frontal. The lows are expected to follow the\npattern of upper 30s/ low 40s pre-frontal, and in the mid 30s\npost-frontal. \n\nConfidence: High confidence for a relatively clear and pleasant\nweekend. Medium confidence in the polar jet bringing \nprecipitation and cold temperatures. Low confidence in \nprecipitation type and timing of the polar jet.",
